WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:04.493
- [Bob Kendrick:] We're talking

00:00:04.493 --> 00:00:06.640
about some of the
greatest baseball players

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:08.863
to ever put on a baseball uniform.

00:00:47.291 --> 00:00:50.830
- During the late 1800s,
during the time of Civil War,

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:52.188
we knew that Major League
Baseball was a sport

00:00:53.347 --> 00:00:55.665
that was really building their foundation.

00:00:55.665 --> 00:00:58.710
We just finally formed
the U.S colored troops.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.150
And when they were in camp,

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:02.030
what were they doing for entertainment?

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:05.373
Getting the wood, a rock,
and they would play baseball.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:09.560
- Here we are fighting in a Civil War,

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:14.203
and yet could not play what
was America's national pastime.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:16.940
- During the late 1800s,

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.820
they didn't actually have Black leagues.

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:21.720
What they had is like regional teams.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:24.560
So there was Black
baseball being played right

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.360
along with everybody else.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.770
Right from the beginning,
there was a color line.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:30.660
And part of the reason
there was a color line is

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.251
because the Black teams
were so outstanding

00:01:33.251 --> 00:01:35.780
that they knew that they would be defeated

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:37.160
if they brought them into the league.

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.740
So they immediately banned those teams.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:43.530
Essentially, they began to
play their game in isolation.

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:44.363
- When we go back

00:01:44.363 --> 00:01:47.060
and look at the early
era of Black baseball,

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:48.260
it was pretty haphazard.

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:55.450
You had several folks of dark
skin who had made their way

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:59.300
into what we now know as
Major League Baseball.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:01.670
Moses Fleetwood Walker was,

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:05.758
as we kind of recognize, the
first known Black player to

00:02:05.758 --> 00:02:09.220
play on what would be
considered a major league team.

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:11.960
Fleetwood Walker was
a bare-handed catcher.

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:12.793
- Tony Mullane,

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:14.574
who was a white pitcher,

00:02:14.574 --> 00:02:18.640
didn't want to receive signs
from Moses Fleetwood Walker.

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:20.650
So Fleetwood Walker caught all year,

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:22.860
not knowing when a curveball was coming.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.081
- And it didn't last very long before guys

00:02:25.081 --> 00:02:27.100
like Adrian "Cap" Anson

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:29.530
formed what we call quote, unquote,

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:33.370
"a gentleman's agreement"
that ultimately banned Blacks

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:36.870
from playing on white major league teams.

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:38.300
And that agreement would stay

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:40.610
in place for the next six decades.

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:45.070
- In 1887, there was an
attempt to form a major league

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:46.640
of Black baseball.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:50.120
It didn't really have the
organization and the finances.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:52.050
I think they played 13 games

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:53.430
and then they were done.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:54.750
- But the movement was there.

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:55.860
The concept was there.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.890
The idea was there that
people would support

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:00.550
an organization like this.

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:02.810
- The "History of Colored Base Ball"

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:04.810
by Sol White was very instrumental

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:08.740
in covering a period of
baseball that we have no way

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:11.510
of knowing more about because those people

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:15.360
from the early 1900s
have long been deceased.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:16.760
The book was groundbreaking

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:19.870
in that it introduced ballplayers
that we never heard of.

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:21.740
From his book, we learned about Pete Hill,

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:23.580
who's in the Hall of Fame.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:24.930
Emmett Bowman.

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:27.390
So many great ballplayers
that we didn't know

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:29.200
about are documented.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:31.750
- This is the first
Black man who's actually

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:35.890
chronicling the history of
African American baseball.

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:38.356
Baseball was truly Black America's game

00:03:38.356 --> 00:03:40.633
in that early part of the century.

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:43.470
- Everyone talks about that

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:45.410
Blacks couldn't play in the major leagues.

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:47.900
But what folks don't recognize is

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:50.640
that African American teams would play

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:52.200
against barnstorming teams

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.380
from the major leagues,
from the minor leagues.

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:56.870
So Blacks and whites
played against each other

00:03:56.870 --> 00:04:00.120
before Jackie Robinson
integrated baseball.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:02.259
- If we really want to
get honest about it,

00:04:02.259 --> 00:04:05.170
they knew these guys could play.

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:08.830
But because of society and America,

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:10.540
they couldn't find a place for them

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:13.210
because the racism was more important

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:14.870
than the national game.

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:15.830
- In that book,

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:18.410
one chapter was written
by Andrew "Rube" Foster.

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:21.340
- You can't tell the
story of the Negro league

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:23.670
without starting with the man,

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:26.550
Andrew "Rube" Foster, the father-

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:28.570
- The father of Black baseball.

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:31.340
- Whether we're talking Black
baseball, white baseball

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:33.150
blue, green, red, yellow,

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:38.150
is the greatest baseball mind
this sport has ever seen.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:41.799
- Rube Foster played the game.

00:04:41.799 --> 00:04:43.920
- Rube was a dominant yet dynamic pitcher.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:45.870
- He is credited with having invented

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:48.400
what we now know to be the screwball.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:50.240
- It is recorded that he won

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:52.390
44 straight games in a season.

00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:55.750
Just imagine that compared
to today's pitcher.

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:59.420
He actually got his nickname
Rube from an exhibition game.

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:01.850
He went up against Hall
of Famer Rube Waddell,

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:02.890
and he actually beat him.

00:05:02.890 --> 00:05:05.080
So his teammates started calling him Rube,

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:07.317
and that nickname stuck with him.

00:05:07.317 --> 00:05:10.540
- Rube Foster became a manager.

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:14.390
1907, he became manager of
the Chicago Leland Giants.

00:05:14.390 --> 00:05:17.420
His team was one of the
greatest teams in America.

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:19.860
Rube Foster became an owner.

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:22.600
- He literally went to
owner Frank Leland and said,

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:25.240
"I want you to give up all
of your controlling rights."

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:28.340
Mr. Leland refused, as you
would think any owner would.

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:29.857
But Rube at that point in time just said,

00:05:29.857 --> 00:05:31.920
"OK, then I quit."

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:33.460
And that's what he did; he quit.

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:35.680
He took Mr. Leland to court

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:37.660
for the naming rights
of the Leland Giants,

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:38.830
and he ultimately won.

00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:39.950
Rube developed

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:42.520
and created his own team
as the Leland Giants.

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:44.670
And that's how he became
owner of the team.

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:51.087
- Around 1919, Rube Foster
started to travel around

00:05:51.087 --> 00:05:56.087
and meet with different owners
of teams in the major cities.

00:05:56.660 --> 00:05:58.190
And so they came together

00:05:58.190 --> 00:06:01.430
in the Paseo YMCA, and they realized

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:04.550
that, "OK, we can pull
this off in the West."

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:06.960
- There were some efforts
to create a Negro league,

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:08.370
but they had failed.

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:13.260
We didn't get that real
magic formula until 1920,

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:16.393
when Rube Foster forms
the Negro National League.

